it has been two years now since the  January 6th attack on the U.S Capitol on  Friday lawmakers held a ceremony on  Capitol Hill and the White House present  and at the White House President Biden  marked the solemn occasion by presenting  medals to 14 Americans for their  exemplary contributions to democracy  during the 2020 election and on January  6. Congressional correspondent Nicole  Killian spoke with three of the  recipients  in an emotional White House ceremony in  history we'll remember your names  and remember your courage President  Biden bestowed the presidential citizens  medal to those who defended democracy  including D.C metropolitan police  officer Daniel Hodges former U.S Capitol  Police Sergeant aquilino Grinnell and  Capitol Police Officer Harry Dunn what  does it mean to you to receive an honor  like this I was left completely  speechless on January 6 we just did our  job that's wild  um yeah I don't know what to say it's  it's crazy I'm honored obviously I am  humble and honored to to receive it but  to tell you the truth I didn't do it  because I wanted a medal I'd rather have  my life back  two years later the images of January 6  are still hard to escape I responded  from the East side uh we heard radio  calls screaming officers just need helps  and helps and help  once I got out of the building I looked  down it was chaos on my way up I was  attacked we were all were attacked I was  hit in the head with a metal pole punch  kicks beating  you can't look at this scene and not  think of it anymore  what is life like for you now it's very  much the same and very different as  before January 6 2021 it's  it's different because that day changed  so much  for me  and for the country my whole life had  changed Sergeant Canal who was brutally  beaten during the riot left the U.S  Capitol Police last month after the  physical and mental trauma became too  much to bear I could do a lot of things  with my left arm but I still had some  type of limitation mentally I'm still  triggered sometimes depending on what  was the issue but removing myself from  the the triggers of being here at the  Capitol every day  allowed me to to heal faster the three  men have formed a brother-like bond  since the attack knowing other officers  are going through what you went through  and have the same thoughts and feelings  about what happened it's it's a big help  because otherwise you just feel alone  it's frightening to think about how  close we were they were the first  witnesses to testify before the January  6th select committee and sat in the  front row during almost every hearing do  you feel like you've gotten some sense  of justice two years later I mean I'm  grateful for the Genesis committee they  uncover a lot of evidence there have  been prosecutions of people but at the  January 6 hearing showed us that it went  much higher than just protesters getting  out of hand  so we can't let people forget I do  believe that people have gotten tired  about tired of this  um hell I'm tired of it I I don't want  to be out here every day  fighting for accountability and Justice  with my my partners here but it needs to  be done holding the line again for CBS  Saturday morning Nicole Killian the  capital  and the thing is it will never leave  them because those are scars that stay  forever and some have as he did have  left the profession entirely I've heard  stories of people working at department  stores or in you know or in coffee shops  because the triggers are just too much  for them to bear and in many other spots  this week the anniversary sort of got  overshadowed by all the drama that was  playing out even though it was the exact  same place yeah
 

